{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Six days you shall eat unleavened bread.</b> And in another place (Exodus 12) it says ״שִׁבְעַת יָמִים״? Seven from the old produce and six from the new (Sifrei; Menachot 66b); another interpretation — it teaches about eating matzah on the seventh day that it is not an obligation, and from here you learn for the six days — for the seventh was included in the general rule and came out of the general rule to teach that eating matzah on it is not an obligation but optional, and it did not come out to teach about itself, but to teach about the entire general rule it came out: just as the seventh is optional, so all of them are optional — except for the first night, which Scripture fixed as an obligation, as it says (Exodus 12) ״בָּעֶרֶב תֹּאכְלוּ מַצֹּת״ (Mekhilta; Pesachim 120a)." "<b>A solemn assembly to YHWH your Elohim.</b> Restrain yourself from labor; another interpretation — a gathering for food and drink — the language of ״נַעְצְרָה נָּא אוֹתָךְ״ (Judges 13)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:47:21.730235650Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}